@page "/security"

<PageTitle>Security and Data Use | It's a Budget</PageTitle>
<HeadContent>
    <meta name="description" content="How It's a Budget uses and protects account, transaction, authentication, and Plaid-connected data." />
</HeadContent>

<article class="public-page">
    <p class="public-page-kicker">SECURITY</p>
    <h1>Security and Data Use</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        Financial records deserve deliberate controls. It's a Budget is designed
        to keep private application data behind authenticated, authorized access.
    </p>

    <h2>Access controls</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Production traffic uses HTTPS.</li>
        <li>Authentication cookies are HTTP-only and configured for secure production use.</li>
        <li>Application permissions and shared-budget roles restrict data access.</li>
        <li>Administrative actions require an active administrator account.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Connected financial data</h2>
    <p>
        Plaid Link handles financial-institution credential entry. It's a Budget
        does not receive or store those institution passwords. Access tokens
        used to maintain connections are server-side secrets and are not sent
        to the browser.
    </p>
    <p>
        Imported transaction data is staged and reconciled. Suggested matches
        require review before they change the checkbook, and account mappings
        are checked against the signed-in user and shared-budget permissions.
    </p>

    <h2>Operations</h2>
    <p>
        The application records diagnostic events needed to detect failures and
        investigate security issues. Backups and restore checks support
        recovery. Secrets are supplied through server configuration rather than
        committed to public application source.
    </p>

    <h2>Responsible reporting</h2>
    <p>
        If you believe you found a vulnerability, do not access or alter other
        users' information. Preserve the minimum evidence needed and use the
        <a href="/contact">contact instructions</a> to report it privately.
    </p>

    <h2>Scope</h2>
    <p>
        No list of safeguards can guarantee absolute security. This page
        describes the application's current approach and is not a certification
        or promise that every attack can be prevented.
    </p>
</article>
